  "strategy": "1. **Approach and rationale**:\n    I wish to propose a fundamentally different optimization technique than the provided program. Instead of using multiple-length optimization and fallback sequences, I will implement a spectral extreme optimization method that focuses on the energy concentration properties of optimal sequences. The core idea is to minimize the peak autocorrelation while maintaining high normalized energy, which aligns with the objective function J(a) = 2*n*M / (sum(a))^2 but requires a stronger mathematical foundation.\n\n    Often, optimal sequences are found to be concentrated in certain frequency bands. We will seek to structure the sequence optimization by controlling these spectral properties directly. The justification is that by designing the sequence according to its spectral characteristics, we can more efficiently navigate the optimization space - avoiding random explorations and instead focusing on constructive changes that directly correlate with the objective function.\n\n    This method is energetically advantageous because it:\n    - Reduces the number of candidate sequences needed\n    - Provides more structured gradient information\n    - Transfers focus from coefficient brute-force adjustment to well-defined spectral domain optimization\n    - Is theoretically grounded in the optics of sequence transform properties\n\n2. **Mathematical specification**:\n    We will represent the sequence in the frequency domain using the discrete Fourier transform (DFT).\n\n    Let F(k) = \u2211_{j=0}^{n-1} a_j * e^{-(2\u03c0i j k / n)} for k = 0 to n/2 (ensuring non-negativity properties)\n\n    Then we convert F to the autocorrelation spectrum A(k) = |F(k)|^2\n\n    The objective is to minimize J(a) = 2*n * max_{k=0 to n-1} A(k) / (sum(a))^2\n\n    Constraints must preserve:\n    - Non-negativity of sequence coefficients\n    - Stability of transformations\n    - Phase alignment (to maintain real responses)\n\n    Forbidden configurations will include:\n    - Entropy-based lossy collapses\n    - Unsupported frequency band plethoras\n    - Cosine insertions exceeding normalized bounds\n\n3. **Implementation procedure**:\n    - All spectrum computations must sample using the FFT (fast Fourier transform) for efficiency\n    - Normalize sequences to the same power before evaluation (-\u221e occurs when power matches)\n    - Apply frequency domain filtering to target the optimal bandpass\n    - Recover sequences from spectral \u2264 1 = |F|^2 domain\n    - Use M\u00f6bius invariance for projection scaling\n    - Implement spectral intensity constraints via Jacobian-analogs\n    \n    def optimize_spectral_energy():\n        Initialize F with normalized coefficients corresponding to a bandpass sequence\n        For each frequency component:\n            if F(frequency) > 0.5 * intensity:\n                apply_jacobian_damping to coefficients\n            else:\n                zero_suppress coefficient if below threshold\n                \n        # Then evaluate J from the resulting sequence\n\n    But note: J calculation requires FFT convolution which may not be consistent with spectral representation.\n\n    Correction: we can compute convolution cost directly from spectrum representations. The linear convolution maxima will be determined by the spectral zeros.\n\n4. **Search choices and useful variations**:\n    - Primary spectral window: frequencies that maximize modal energy while staying below bandwidth thresholds\n    - Energy compaction factor: min/max bandwidth ratio of 0.2 to 1.0 (Jamesian default)\n    - Damping coefficient schedules: exponential decay with base 0.6\n    - Bandpass frequency profile: Gaussian-like near zero to cut off higher modes\n\n    Multiple runners will be maintained but with different damping profiles. The primary will attempt:\n    - Forward transform with windowed damping\n    - Coefficient adjustment based on spectral inhomogeneities\n    - Skipping of unnecessary cycles\n\n    The procedure is decided to be computationally frugal by maintaining a direct mapping between spectral coefficients and sequence components.\n\n5. **Computebudget and robustness**:\n    - FFT computations dominate (complexity O(n log n))\n    - Energy filtering has linear cost\n    - Jacobian scaling has negligible overhead if n\u2264500\n\n    Robustness measures we'll implement:\n    - Spectral energy consistency checks\n    - Mean squared error of bandpass approximation\n    - Virtual damping allocation based on inversely proportional time limits\n\n    Budget breakdown:\n    - Forward FFT transforms: 5n < registers\n    - Energy normalization loop: 0.2 * (n^2)\n    - Damping parameterizations: 0.1 * M^(n-1)\n    - Best candidate: first periodic sigma stabilizer then worst-case damping\n\n6. **Validation and return contract**:\n    To be valid, the returned sequence must satisfy:\n    - Non-negative coefficients\n    - Sum \u2265 0.01\n    - Real-valued in time domain\n    - Evaluate to finite J value \u2264 1.5027\n\n    The spectral optimization procedure is verified by:\n    - Evaluating J exactly after each transformation step\n    - Sanitizing coefficients with the standardized clipping [0,1000]\n    - Reconstructing the sequence from spectral domain\n    - Storing the best valid candidate\n\n    Evaluation penalty adjustments (if time remaining < 0.05*n) abort with no improvement.",
